<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">War and march · Anthem · 1794 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Le Chant du départ</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A French marching song of 1794, called the brother of the Marseillaise. It is still played for the President of France.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Marie-Joseph Chénier</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Étienne Méhul</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>French Republican soldiers; later the French Empire and the French Republic</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>France</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1794; first performed 14 July 1794</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>War and march · Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Marie-Joseph Chénier, music by Étienne Méhul (1794))</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chant-du-depart">Le Chant du départ</a>&quot; (&quot;The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of Departure&quot;) is a war song of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, with music by Étienne Méhul and words by Marie-Joseph Chénier, written in 1794. Its original title was &quot;Anthem to Liberty&quot;; Robespierre changed it to the present one. It was first performed on 14 July 1794, and 18,000 copies of the music were at once printed and sent out to the army. Republican soldiers called it &quot;the brother of the Marseillaise&quot;.</p><p>The song is a musical tableau: each of its seven stanzas is sung by a different character or group. In the first a deputy rallies soldiers to fight for the Republic; in the second a mother offers her son's life to the fatherland. The fourth is sung by children in praise of Joseph Agricol Viala and Joseph Bara, boys of 15 and 13 who had died for Revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. By the legend, Bara was surrounded by royalist Vendeans and ordered to cheer for Louis XVII, cheered for the Republic instead and was killed on the spot; Viala was shot while trying to sabotage an enemy bridge.</p><p>It became the official anthem of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a>. The French Army still sings it, and it was sung during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>. Valéry Giscard d'Estaing used it as his campaign song in the presidential election of 1974 and, as president, often had troops play it together with &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-marseillaise">La Marseillaise</a>&quot;. Today it is the official presidential anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the unofficial regional anthem of French Guiana.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A patriotic call to leave home and fight for the Republic, in which a series of voices, a deputy, a mother, children and others, each give their reason to defend the country. The mood is solemn and stirring, a march of farewell and duty.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">French</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">French</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">La Victoire, en chantant, nous ouvre la barrière ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Victory, singing, opens the barrier to us;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">La Liberté guide nos pas ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Liberty guides our steps;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Et du nord au midi, la trompette guerrière</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And from the north to the south, the warlike trumpet</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">A sonné l’heure des combats.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Has sounded the hour of battle.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Tremblez, ennemis de la France,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Tremble, enemies of France,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Rois ivres de sang et d’orgueil !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Kings drunk with blood and pride!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Le peuple souverain s’avance ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The sovereign people advances;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Tyrans, descendez au cercueil.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Tyrants, go down into the coffin.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">La république nous appelle ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The republic calls us;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Sachons vaincre, ou sachons périr :</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us know how to conquer, or know how to perish:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Un Français doit vivre pour elle ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A Frenchman must live for her;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pour elle, un Français doit mourir.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For her, a Frenchman must die.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">De nos yeux maternels ne craignez point les larmes ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Do not fear the tears of our mothers' eyes;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Loin de nous de lâches douleurs !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Far from us be cowardly griefs!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Nous devons triompher, quand vous prenez les armes :</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We must triumph, when you take up arms:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">C’est aux rois à verser des pleurs.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It is for kings to shed tears.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Nous vous avons donné la vie :</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We gave you life:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Guerriers, elle n’est plus à vous :</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Warriors, it is no longer yours:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Tous vos jours sont à la patrie ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>All your days belong to the fatherland;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Elle est votre mère avant nous.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>She is your mother before us.</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 60 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Que le fer paternel arme la main des braves ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let the father's sword arm the hand of the brave;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Songez à nous aux champs de Mars :</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Think of us on the fields of Mars:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Consacrez dans le sang des rois et des esclaves</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Consecrate in the blood of kings and of slaves</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Le fer béni par vos vieillards ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The sword blessed by your old men;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Et, rapportant sous la chaumière</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And, bringing back under the thatched roof</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Des blessures et des vertus,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Wounds and virtues,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Venez fermer notre paupière,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Come and close our eyelids,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Quand les tyrans ne seront plus.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When the tyrants are no more.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">De Barra, de Viala, le sort nous fait envie ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Of Barra, of Viala, we envy the fate;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ils sont morts, mais ils ont vaincu ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>They are dead, but they have conquered;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Le lâche accablé d’ans n’a point connu la vie :</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The coward weighed down with years has never known life:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Qui meurt pour le peuple a vécu.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Whoever dies for the people has lived.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Vous êtes vaillans, nous le sommes ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You are valiant, so are we;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Guidez-nous contre les tyrans ?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Lead us against the tyrants?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Les républicains sont des hommes ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Republicans are men;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Les esclaves sont des enfans.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Slaves are children.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Partez, vaillans époux, les combats sont vos fêtes ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Go, valiant husbands, battles are your feasts;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Partez, modèles des guerriers ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Go, models of warriors;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Nous cueillerons des fleurs pour en ceindre vos têtes ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We will gather flowers to wreathe your heads with them;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Nos mains tresseront vos lauriers.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Our hands will weave your laurels.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Et si le temple de mémoire</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And if the temple of memory</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">S’ouvrait à vos mânes vainqueurs,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Should open to your victorious shades,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Nos voix chanteront votre gloire,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Our voices will sing your glory,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Et nos flancs portent vos vengeurs.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And our wombs carry your avengers.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Et nous, sœurs des héros, nous qui de l’hyménée</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And we, sisters of heroes, we who of marriage</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ignorons les aimables nœuds,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Know nothing of the lovely knots,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Si, pour s’unir un jour à notre destinée,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>If, to join one day with our destiny,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Les citoyens forment des vœux,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The citizens make vows,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Qu’ils reviennent dans nos murailles,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let them come back within our walls,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Beaux de gloire et de liberté,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Fair with glory and with liberty,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Et que leur sang, dans les batailles,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And let their blood, in the battles,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ait coulé pour l’égalité.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Have flowed for equality.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Sur ce fer, devant Dieu, nous jurons à nos pères,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>On this sword, before God, we swear to our fathers,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">À nos épouses, à nos sœurs,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To our wives, to our sisters,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">À nos représentans, à nos fils, à nos mères,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To our representatives, to our sons, to our mothers,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">D’anéantir les oppresseurs.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To annihilate the oppressors.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">En tous lieux, dans la nuit profonde</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In every place, into the deep night</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Plongeant l’infâme royauté,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Plunging infamous royalty,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Les Français donneront au monde</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The French will give the world</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Et la paix et la liberté.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Both peace and liberty.</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Marie-Joseph Chénier (1794). Text from <a href="https://fr.wikisource.org/wiki/Le_Chant_du_d%C3%A9part_(texte_seul)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (10)</summary><ul><li>line 1.1: 'la barrière' is the barrier or gate (of the lists, or the frontier) that Victory opens.</li><li>line 2.1: literally 'Of our maternal eyes do not fear the tears'; this stanza is sung by mothers.</li><li>line 3.1: 'le fer' (iron) is the sword, here the fathers' sword; this stanza is sung by old men.</li><li>line 3.2: 'champs de Mars': the fields of Mars, the battlefields (also the parade ground in Paris).</li><li>line 4.1: Joseph Bara (spelt Barra) and Joseph Agricol Viala, boy soldiers of the Republic killed in 1793.</li><li>line 4.6: the question mark is in the source text.</li><li>line 5.6: 'mânes', the shades or spirits of the dead.</li><li>line 5.8: 'flancs', literally flanks, here the wombs; 'portent' is present tense in the text.</li><li>line 6.2: 'hyménée', marriage (Hymen); 'nœuds', the bonds or knots of marriage.</li><li>'vaillans', 'enfans', 'représentans': the old spelling of vaillants, enfants, représentants.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
